messages:
marta (DevEx): Hey — hitting a weird one. Trying to run the Node SDK from inside a customer's VPC that uses a corporate HTTP proxy + private Redwood endpoint. If I set baseUrl to https://private.redwood.cust and stream a long response, the stream cuts with ECONNRESET after ~30s. Anyone seen this pattern?

kevin (SDK): How are you configuring the proxy/agent and the client? Most timeouts like that are proxy or LB idle timeouts. Small checklist: keepAlive on the agent, proper Host header for SNI, and proxy_read_timeout on the proxy.

marta (DevEx): Minimal snippet I'm using:```js
const { RedwoodClient } = require('@redwood/inference');
const HttpsProxyAgent = require('https-proxy-agent');

const proxyAgent = new HttpsProxyAgent(process.env.HTTPS_PROXY);
const client = new RedwoodClient({
  baseUrl: 'https://private.redwood.cust',
  transportOptions: { agent: proxyAgent },
  timeout: 0, // stream should be indefinite
});

const stream = await client.stream({ input: 'long_chat' });
stream.on('data', chunk => console.log(chunk.toString()));
```
It works from my laptop through the same proxy, but not from inside their VPC.

kevin (SDK): A few things to try: 1) set proxyAgent.options.keepAlive = true (or create agent with { keepAlive: true }) so sockets don't close; 2) explicitly set headers: { Connection: 'keep-alive', Host: 'private.redwood.cust' } so the proxy preserves SNI/host multiplexing; 3) some proxies terminate long upstream responses if they think it's idle — increase proxy_read_timeout. Example:```js
const proxyAgent = new HttpsProxyAgent({
  host: process.env.PROXY_HOST,
  port: process.env.PROXY_PORT,
  keepAlive: true,
});
const client = new RedwoodClient({
  baseUrl: 'https://private.redwood.cust',
  transportOptions: { agent: proxyAgent, headers: { Connection: 'keep-alive', Host: 'private.redwood.cust' } },
});
```
Also ensure the proxy isn't doing HTTP/1.0 and dropping Connection headers.

sam (eng-infra): +1 on proxy timeouts. For infra side: if their proxy is NGINX we usually bump proxy_read_timeout and proxy_send_timeout to >120s. If there's an ALB in front of it, check the idle timeout (ALB default 60s). Also watch for TLS re-negotiation requirements — if their NAT or firewall kills idle TCP after 30s, need keepalive probes.

kevin (SDK): Another gotcha — if the client cert or mTLS is required for private endpoints, ensure the upstream TLS handshake is using the same hostname as the cert (so Host header and SNI must match). If Host header is stripped by the proxy, the cert validation can fail intermittently.

marta (DevEx): Good call, their infra team confirmed a downstream load balancer with 30s idle TCP kill. They increased it and I added keepAlive on the agent + Connection header. Also had to set host header to the original host so server cert validation passed. After changes stream stayed up for >5m.

kevin (SDK): Nice. We should add a short note in the SDK docs: "If using private endpoints behind HTTP proxies/load-balancers, enable agent keepAlive, set Connection: keep-alive, and ensure proxy timeouts and ALB idle timeouts exceed expected stream idle periods. Also ensure SNI/Host header passes through for mTLS." 

marta (DevEx): I'll open a small docs PR with the exact config and a Node snippet. Also adding a troubleshooting checklist: proxy keepalive, proxy_read_timeout, ALB idle timeout, host/SNI, and confirm any mTLS certs.

infra-bot: deploy:infra-proxy-config/patched applied to staging :white_check_mark:

sam (eng-infra): FYI if anyone else hits this we sometimes recommend a lightweight TCP keepalive at the OS level too (sysctl net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_*), but app-level keepAlive + proxy timeout is usually sufficient.

marta (DevEx): thanks all — marking resolved for now, will link the PR when ready. :pray:
